## The function (`phone.js`)

```js
const EXT_RE = /\s*(?:,|;|ext\.?|extension|x|#)\s*(\d{1,6})\s*$/i;

function normalizePhone(input) {
  if (typeof input !== "string") return { ok: false, error: "not a string" };

  let s = input.trim();
  let ext = null;
  const m = s.match(EXT_RE);
  if (m) { ext = m[1]; s = s.slice(0, m.index); }

  // Only digits, spaces, and common separators (+ only as a leading char) are allowed.
  if (!/^\+?[\d\s().\-–]+$/.test(s)) return { ok: false, error: "contains invalid characters" };

  const hadPlus = s.startsWith("+");
  let digits = s.replace(/\D/g, "");

  if (hadPlus && !digits.startsWith("1")) return { ok: false, error: "non-US/Canada country code" };
  if (digits.length === 11 && digits[0] === "1") digits = digits.slice(1);
  else if (hadPlus && digits.length !== 11) return { ok: false, error: `expected 11 digits after +, got ${digits.length}` };

  if (digits.length !== 10) return { ok: false, error: `expected 10 digits, got ${digits.length}` };

  // NANP rules: area code and exchange start with 2-9.
  if (!/^[2-9]\d{2}[2-9]\d{6}$/.test(digits)) return { ok: false, error: "invalid area code or exchange (cannot start with 0 or 1)" };

  return { ok: true, e164: "+1" + digits, ext };
}
```

## What it does

- **Good input:** it returns `{ ok: true, e164: "+15552014433", ext: null }`.
  - It handles all four of your formats, plus `1-555-201-4433` and `+1 (555) 201-4433`.
  - It also accepts a trailing extension like `x12` or `ext. 345`. The extension comes back in `ext` and is kept out of the E.164 value.
- **Bad input:** it never throws. It returns `{ ok: false, error }`, so you can log or show the reason.

| Input | Result |
|---|---|
| `"12345"` | `expected 10 digits, got 5` |
| `"+44 20 7946 0958"` | `non-US/Canada country code` |
| `"055-201-4433"` | `invalid area code or exchange (cannot start with 0 or 1)` |
| `"abc"` | `contains invalid characters` |
| `null` | `not a string` |

## Design choices

- **No single regex does the whole job.** I strip the separators and count digits instead. Only the final validity check is a regex, `^[2-9]\d{2}[2-9]\d{6}$`, which enforces the US/Canada rule that area codes and exchanges can't start with 0 or 1. Without it, `5552014433` and `0001234567` would both pass.
- **Letters are rejected, not stripped.** Otherwise `555-CALL-NOW` would quietly turn into a wrong number.
- **Too many or too few digits is an error.** I don't truncate or pad, because a wrong number stored silently is worse than a rejected one.

## Limits

- **US/Canada only.** For international numbers, use [`libphonenumber-js`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/libphonenumber-js). Its `parsePhoneNumberFromString(input, "US")` covers every country, and it also checks whether the number is actually assigned. This function only checks format, so a number like `555-201-4433` passes even though it may not be a real line.
- **Extension edge case.** An input like `555 201 4433 5` isn't read as an extension. It fails the digit count.

To run the tests, use `node --test` in that directory.

